      "section_title": "Graph Mapper: Maps a number of values using a function.",
      "section_number": 3,
      "teaching_text": "3- Graph Mapper: Maps a number of values using a function.\r\n\r\n- Using Multiple INPUTS:\r\n- Take the previous example and input a seconf value.\r\n\r\n- Get a Panel and watch the results.\r\n\r\n- Important: The resulting data depends on the order of the values you input. Try to use the \"Merge\" component to keep track of that order of inputs.\r\n\r\n- Use a \"Range\" of values to input more values.\r\n- Notice that the \"Domain\" D INPUT of the \"Range\" component comes with a default domain between 0 & 1.\r\n- Also notive that the default number of steps is 10, illustrated by the N INPUT.\r\n\r\n- You can change both the D & N values by contructing a new \"Domain\" and using an integer slider.\r\n\r\n>> Be Careful! If the domain range goes beyond the Graph Mapper's domain, there will be missed output results!",
      "teaching_panel_id": "C301",
      "additional_teaching_panels": [
        {
          "id": "C300",
          "text": "2- Graph Mapper: Maps a number of values using a function.\r\n\r\n- Make a number slider between 0-1\r\n\r\n- Make a \"Graph Mapper\":\r\n1- Right-Click on the \"Graph Mapper\" and click on \"Graph Types\" and choose \"Bezier\".\r\n2- Bring a Panel and watch the output of the Graph Mapper.\r\n\r\n- Notice the red line illustrating the INPUT value (along the X axis).\r\n- The Y value is the mapped value of X, following the bezier function.\r\n\r\n\r\n- Copy the Graph Mapper and start manipulating the X & Y domains by double-clicking on the Graph Mapper."
        }
      ],
      "sub_annotations": [
        {
          "id": "C207",
          "text": "If the domain of values is greater than the domain of the Graph Mapper, the Graph Mapper will only affect the values within its own domain.",
          "topic": "If the domain of values is greater than the domain of the Graph Mapper, the Graph Mapper will only affect the values within its own domain.",
