    {
      "column_index": 4,
      "header_id": "C231",
      "x_range": [
        4643.9,
        6717.1
      ],
      "section_title": "Graph Mapper: Maps a number of values using a function.",
      "section_number": 5,
      "teaching_text": "5- Graph Mapper: Maps a number of values using a function.\r\n\r\n- Practical Example:\r\n\r\n- Use the previous Graph Mapper definition.\r\n- Construct a Point.\r\n- Feed the same INPUT range of the Graph Mapper to the X INPUT of the Point.\r\n- Feed the OUTPUT of the Graph Mapper into the Z INPUT of the Point.\r\n- Check the results in Perspective view in Rhino while selecting the Constructed Point.\r\n- Deconstruct the Point to seperate the X,Y & Z values.\r\n- Build a new Circle.\r\n- Construct a new Point and feed its Z with the deconstructed Point above Z value.\r\n- Feed these new Z values into the Plane INPUT of the Circle.\r\n- Feed the X OUTPUT of the Deconstructed Point into the Radius INPUT of the Circle.\r\n- Check the results in Perspective view in Rhino while selecting the Circle.\r\n- Final touch: Loft the circles together.\r\n\r\n- If you have a low quality preview of the loft, click on \"Preview Mesh Quality\" at the Top Right corner of your viewport and select \"High Quality\".\r\n\r\n- Change the type of the Graph Mapper and check out the results!",
      "teaching_panel_id": "C302",
      "additional_teaching_panels": [],
      "sub_annotations": [],
      "concepts_taught": [
