    {
      "column_index": 6,
      "header_id": "C51",
      "x_range": [
        10030.8,
        12039.9
      ],
      "section_title": "Paneling",
      "section_number": 6,
      "teaching_text": "6- Paneling: \r\n\r\n1- Model a non-planar surface in Rhino\r\n\r\n2- Use the \"Divide Domain²\" component to divide the domain of the surface in both U & V directions.\r\n\r\n3- Use the \"isoTrim\" component to make sub-surfaces based on the divide domain² information.\r\n\r\n4- Use the \"DeConstruct Brep\" to extract:\r\na- Faces (surfaces)\r\nb- Edges\r\nc- Vertecies\r\n\r\n5- Now you have a panelized surface.",
      "teaching_panel_id": "C297",
      "additional_teaching_panels": [
        {
          "id": "C50",
          "text": "Often, what you want to code in Grasshopper has already been done by others before you.\r\nIt would be interesting to make a quick online research and find helpful resources.\r\nOne of the main online resources for Grasshopper definitions is:\r\nhttps://discourse.mcneel.com/c/grasshopper/2\r\n\r\nOR:\r\n\r\nJust search in Google: \"Grasshopper [WHAT I WANT TO DESIGN]\" and check out the results."
        }
      ],
      "sub_annotations": [
        {
          "id": "C49",
          "text": "The following bit of code below is inspired by the source mentioned here.\r\n\r\nThe main feature of this definition is that we can control:\r\n1- Radius of effect\r\n2- Strength of effect\r\n\r\nOnline source:\r\nhttps://generativelandscapes.wordpress.com/2014/08/21/multiple-point-vector-field-example-6-2",
          "topic": "The following bit of code below is inspired by the source mentioned here.",
          "pos": [
            10026.4,
            342.9
          ]
        }
      ],
